<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tavoliere delle Puglie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tavoliere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tavoliere delle Puglie is a broad plain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> around the city of Foggia, the second largest plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the Po valley. In antiquity it was the land of the Daunians, and in later centuries it was used mainly as winter pasture for flocks driven down from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> mountains. Since the 20th century it has been one of Italy's chief wheat-growing areas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tavoliere-delle-puglie.jpg" alt="Tavoliere delle Puglie" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Attilios at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tavoliere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The plain, whose name means the table of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulias</a>, covers about 3,000 square kilometres and takes up nearly half of the traditional region of Capitanata. It was once a sea bottom. It is bounded by the Daunian Pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> to the west, the Gargano Promontory and the Adriatic Sea to the east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortore">Fortore</a> river to the north and the Ofanto river to the south. In winter the two rivers sometimes flood it, while drought is frequent in summer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farmers lived on the plain more than 7,000 years ago and practised ritual defleshing of their dead, as light cut marks on the bones show, sometimes up to a year after death. They placed the bones in Scaloria Cave, mixed with animal bones, broken pottery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the older practices of farming and river control were lost, and the land became largely marshy and unhealthy. The plain was given over mostly to sheep, which moved along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-tracks across it to the pastures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>. The name Tavoliere itself comes from the Medieval Latin Tabularium, the table on which officials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transhumance">transhumance</a> classified the land set aside for sheep farming.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After extensive drainage works the plain is highly cultivated, with wheat, beet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomatoes</a>, especially around Foggia, and olives and vines that give oils and wines of quality. Its main centres, from north to south, are San Severo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Lucera</a>, Foggia and Cerignola. More than thirty communes lie on the plain, among them Troia, Manfredonia, Ascoli Satriano, Torremaggiore and Ordona, the site of ancient Herdonia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tavoliere delle Puglie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Daunians were the northernmost of the Iapygian peoples of Apulia, settled in the <b>Tavoliere</b> plain and the Gargano from the early 1st millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a></li></ul></section>
